---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.2 — for Loops &amp; The EncounterSim Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topic 2.8 · 90-Minute Design-First Build.</strong> When you know the exact range of
    rooms, floors, or indices to visit, a <code>for</code> loop packs initialization, termination
    condition, and step update into one header. Today you will design and build
    <code>EncounterSim.java</code> — a dungeon simulator that tests accumulators, inclusive bounds,
    streak detection, and mid-run death exits.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (One-Shot Challenge):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/EncounterSim.java" download>EncounterSim.java</a> — starter file with
            Javadocs, method stubs, and a built-in <strong>Dungeon Encounter Simulator Bench</strong>
            in <code>main()</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Read why the game needs each function and what
            its parameters carry, then answer the Guided Design questions on accumulator scope,
            loop bounds, streak resets, and condition ordering.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; test in the sandbox:</strong> Draft your method code in the box,
            click <strong>Copy</strong> to paste into <code>EncounterSim.java</code>, and check the
            live simulator bench with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac EncounterSim.java
java EncounterSim</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (One-Shot Pass):</strong> Once your sandbox results match every walkthrough
        example, unlock <code>EncounterSimCheck.java</code> below and aim to pass all
        <strong>16 checks</strong> on your first run.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Range Accumulation &amp; Boss Multipliers</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>totalScaledXP(int startFloor, int endFloor, int bossMultiplier)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            When a party clears a stretch of dungeon levels from <code>startFloor</code> through
            <code>endFloor</code>, the reward calculator sums up XP across every cleared floor.
            Standard floors give <code>f * 100</code> XP, while every 5th floor (a Boss Floor)
            replaces standard XP with a multiplied reward.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int startFloor</code> — first floor cleared (inclusive).</li>
            <li><code>int endFloor</code> — last floor cleared (inclusive).</li>
            <li><code>int bossMultiplier</code> — XP multiplier on Boss Floors (when <code>f % 5 == 0</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>int</code>):</strong> total XP across <code>startFloor .. endFloor</code>,
                or <code>0</code> if <code>startFloor &gt; endFloor</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>(1, 4, 3)</code> &rarr; <code>100 + 200 + 300 + 400 = 1000</code>.</li>
            <li><code>(1, 5, 3)</code> &rarr; <code>1000 + (5 * 100 * 3) = 2500</code> (Floor 5 is
                included and multiplied, not double-counted!).</li>
            <li><code>(5, 5, 4)</code> &rarr; <code>2000</code>; <code>(6, 3, 2)</code> &rarr; <code>0</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>totalScaledXP</code></h3>
        <p>Plan your accumulator, bounds, and boss branch before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Accumulator Scope &amp; Loop Choice:</strong> Why should this use a
                <code>for</code> loop rather than a <code>while</code> loop? Where must
                <code>int total = 0;</code> be declared—<em>before</em> the loop or <em>inside</em>
                the loop body—and what happens if you accidentally declare it inside?</li>
            <li><strong>Inclusive Bounds &amp; Mutual Exclusion:</strong> Why must the loop test use
                <code>f &lt;= endFloor</code> instead of <code>f &lt; endFloor</code>, and how does
                that same header automatically return <code>0</code> when
                <code>startFloor &gt; endFloor</code>? Why must Boss Floor XP use
                <code>if / else</code> rather than adding standard XP and then adding boss XP?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>EncounterSim.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>totalScaledXP</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>EncounterSim.java</code>, and check Part 1 in <code>java EncounterSim</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Procedural Radar &amp; Threshold Counting</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>countDangerSpikes(int numRooms, int seed, int threshold)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Before a party enters a floor, the dungeon scout computes a deterministic danger rating
            for each room <code>r = 1 .. numRooms</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>int danger = (r * seed + 17) % 100;</code></pre>
        <p>
            Counting how many rooms meet or exceed <code>threshold</code> lets the UI warn players
            about high-threat corridors.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int numRooms</code> — number of rooms (1-based: <code>1 .. numRooms</code>;
                returns <code>0</code> if <code>numRooms &lt;= 0</code>).</li>
            <li><code>int seed</code> — procedural generator seed.</li>
            <li><code>int threshold</code> — inclusive minimum danger rating counted as a spike.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Trace (<code>numRooms = 5, seed = 20</code>)</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li>Rooms 1–5 have danger <code>37, 57, 77, 97, 17</code>.</li>
            <li>With <code>threshold = 57</code> &rarr; <code>3</code> spikes (<code>57 &gt;= 57</code>
                counts!). With <code>threshold = 98</code> &rarr; <code>0</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>countDangerSpikes</code></h3>
        <p>Check your loop index and threshold boundary before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>1-Based Bounds &amp; Threshold Operator:</strong> Why would starting at
                <code>r = 0; r &lt; numRooms</code> compute the wrong danger values compared to
                <code>r = 1; r &lt;= numRooms</code>, and which relational operator checks
                <code>threshold</code> inclusively?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>EncounterSim.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>countDangerSpikes</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>EncounterSim.java</code>, and check Part 2 in <code>java EncounterSim</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Streak Tracking (AP FRQ Favorite)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>longestSafeStreak(int numRooms, int trapCycle, int bossRoom)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Longest-consecutive-streak tracking appears constantly on AP FRQ 1. Walking rooms
            <code>1 .. numRooms</code> (precondition: <code>trapCycle &gt; 0</code>), a room
            <code>r</code> is <strong>unsafe</strong> if <code>r == bossRoom || r % trapCycle == 0</code>,
            and <strong>safe</strong> otherwise. This helper reports the longest uninterrupted run of
            safe rooms.
        </p>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>(10, 4, 9)</code> &rarr; unsafe are <code>4, 8, 9</code>; streaks are
                <code>1..3</code> (3), <code>5..7</code> (3), <code>10</code> (1) &rarr; <code>3</code>.</li>
            <li><code>(10, 6, 2)</code> &rarr; unsafe are <code>2, 6</code>; trailing rooms
                <code>7..10</code> form a streak of <code>4</code> at the very end!</li>
            <li><code>(7, 99, 99)</code> &rarr; all 7 rooms are safe &rarr; <code>7</code>.</li>
            <li><code>(5, 1, 3)</code> &rarr; every room is divisible by <code>1</code> &rarr; <code>0</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>longestSafeStreak</code></h3>
        <p>Design the two state variables and avoid the classic trailing-streak bug:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Local State Variables:</strong> What two integer variables must you declare
                and initialize to <code>0</code> before the loop to track both the active safe run
                and the best run seen so far?</li>
            <li><strong>Trailing-Streak Edge Case:</strong> Suppose a programmer only updates
                <code>maxStreak</code> inside the <em>unsafe</em> branch right before resetting
                <code>currentStreak = 0;</code>. What wrong answer would that return on
                <code>(10, 6, 2)</code> and <code>(7, 99, 99)</code>, and why does updating
                <code>maxStreak</code> inside the <em>safe</em> branch immediately after incrementing
                <code>currentStreak</code> fix both?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>EncounterSim.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>longestSafeStreak</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>EncounterSim.java</code>, and check all four streak cases in
                <code>java EncounterSim</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · AP FRQ Synthesis: Priority Order &amp; Mid-Run Death</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>simulateGauntletHP(int startingHP, int numRooms, int trapCycle, int healCycle)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            A hero enters a gauntlet of rooms <code>1 .. numRooms</code> with
            <code>startingHP</code>. Each room triggers exactly one event based on divisibility, and
            falling to <code>&lt;= 0</code> HP ends the run immediately so later healing fountains
            cannot raise the dead.
        </p>
        <h4>Room Priority Rules &amp; Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><strong>Cursed Fountain</strong> (divisible by BOTH <code>trapCycle</code> and
                <code>healCycle</code>): subtract <code>5</code> HP.</li>
            <li><strong>Trap</strong> (else divisible by <code>trapCycle</code>): subtract
                <code>r * 2</code> HP.</li>
            <li><strong>Healing Fountain</strong> (else divisible by <code>healCycle</code>): add
                <code>6</code> HP.</li>
            <li><strong>Travel Fatigue</strong> (otherwise): subtract <code>1</code> HP.</li>
            <li>If HP drops to <code>&lt;= 0</code> at the end of any room, return <code>0</code>
                immediately! (If <code>numRooms &lt;= 0</code>, return <code>startingHP</code> or
                <code>0</code> if <code>startingHP &lt;= 0</code>.)</li>
            <li>Examples: <code>(20, 6, 2, 3) &rarr; 7</code>;
                <code>(5, 4, 2, 3) &rarr; 0</code> (dies in Room 2);
                <code>(20, 0, 2, 3) &rarr; 20</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>simulateGauntletHP</code></h3>
        <p>Analyze branch priority and mid-loop exit placement:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>FizzBuzz Branch Order:</strong> In <code>(20, 6, 2, 3)</code>, Room 6 is
                divisible by both <code>2</code> and <code>3</code>. What happens if you check
                <code>r % trapCycle == 0</code> <em>above</em> checking both divisors?</li>
            <li><strong>Post-Mortem Healing Edge Case:</strong> Trace <code>(5, 4, 2, 3)</code>
                if a programmer waits until <em>after</em> the <code>for</code> loop finishes to
                check <code>if (hp &lt;= 0) return 0;</code>. What happens in Room 3, and where
                inside the loop must the death check live?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>EncounterSim.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>simulateGauntletHP</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>EncounterSim.java</code>, and run <code>java EncounterSim</code> before
                unlocking <code>EncounterSimCheck.java</code> below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods produce expected results in <code>java EncounterSim</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>EncounterSimCheck.java</code> below and aim for <strong><code>16 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> on your first run</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/for-loops.html">for Loops</a> — counting patterns, scope, accumulators</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/while-loops.html">while vs for</a> — choosing the right loop</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.2" title="4.2 for Loops &amp; The EncounterSim Build — AP CS A" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
